-- ==========================================================================
-- unit1-propositions-proofs.sokonanoda — Unit 1 · Propositions & Proof Terms
--
-- We begin with logic itself, before universes or Sort ever come up: your job
-- here is simply to prove propositions. The ground rules:
--
--   * An exercise is a declaration whose value is `sorry`. Replace `sorry`
--     with your answer, and let the kernel be the judge.
--   * A partial answer is a legitimate state: write the first few `fun`
--     layers, leave the last one as `sorry`, and the remaining goal stays
--     open for you.
--   * Everything you complete here is also valid in official Lean: the
--     grammar you learn is a real subset of Lean 4.
-- ==========================================================================

-- Prop is the world of propositions: True, And a b, and the rest. A proof is a
-- term — a term of type P is a proof of the proposition P. To stay minimal we
-- declare a small logical core with axioms; it is isomorphic to official
-- Lean's And/Or, so nothing you prove here is Lean-specific.

axiom True : Prop
axiom True.intro : True
axiom False : Prop
axiom False.rec : (P : Prop) -> False -> P
axiom And : Prop -> Prop -> Prop
axiom And.intro : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> a -> b -> And a b
axiom And.left : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> And a b -> a
axiom And.right : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> And a b -> b
axiom Or : Prop -> Prop -> Prop
axiom Or.inl : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> a -> Or a b
axiom Or.inr : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> b -> Or a b

-- Not is not an axiom but a definition: Not a expands to a -> False.

def Not : Prop -> Prop := fun (a : Prop) => a -> False

-- Self-test: #reduce unfolds definitions, so Not True becomes True -> False.

#reduce Not True

-- Demo (already written): constructors double as functions — pass the
-- arguments in order and you have a proof.

example : Or True False := Or.inl True False True.intro

-- The correspondence that drives everything: an arrow in a type becomes a
-- `fun` in a value. Writing a proof is therefore writing a run of `fun`s and
-- one final constructor call.

-- Exercise 1 — your very first "proof". The proof term for True already
-- exists; just call it out.
-- soko:hint approach: a proof is a term — a term of type True is a proof of True.
-- soko:hint goal shape: True has no arrows to split; ask what in your environment produces True.
-- soko:hint key piece: True.intro — the constructor of True; it needs no arguments.
theorem true_is_true : True := sorry

-- Exercise 2 — if a and b both hold, you can get And a b.
-- Hint: four layers of fun, finishing with And.intro a b ha hb.
-- soko:hint approach: arrows become fun — build the proof as a chain of funs with one final constructor step.
-- soko:hint goal shape: the outermost layer has four arrows; open with fun (a : Prop) => and re-read the goal after every layer.
-- soko:hint key piece: finish with And.intro, which takes a, b and the two proofs, and assembles And a b.
theorem and_intro_rule : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> a -> b -> And a b := sorry

-- Exercise 3 — pull h apart with And.left/And.right, then put it back together
-- with And.intro, in the opposite order.
-- soko:hint approach: take the And a b you hold and rebuild it the other way round — eliminate, then construct.
-- soko:hint goal shape: three arrows: fun (a : Prop) => fun (b : Prop) => fun (h : And a b) => …, and the final goal is And b a.
-- soko:hint key piece: And.left / And.right each extract one half of h; And.intro b a … reassembles them swapped.
theorem and_swap : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> And a b -> And b a := sorry

-- Exercise 4 — False has no proof (no constructor), yet it is far from
-- useless: from a contradiction you may deduce anything.
-- soko:hint approach: False cannot be proven, but it can be consumed — a contradiction implies any proposition.
-- soko:hint goal shape: two arrows: fun (P : Prop) =>, then consume h : False; the remaining goal is an arbitrary P.
-- soko:hint key piece: False.rec takes a target proposition and a proof of False, and yields a proof of that proposition.
theorem ex_falso : (P : Prop) -> False -> P := sorry

-- Exercise 5 (challenge ★) — And a (Not a) is self-contradictory. Split it and
-- apply the Not a half to the other half. Hint: Not a expands to a -> False.
-- soko:hint approach: And packages a with Not a; Not a expands to a -> False, a function that eats an a.
-- soko:hint goal shape: two layers of fun give you a and h : And a (Not a); the goal is then False itself.
-- soko:hint key piece: And.right yields the Not a half and And.left the a half; apply the former to the latter.
theorem and_not_absurd : (a : Prop) -> And a (Not a) -> False := sorry

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Aside: declaration binders — let the declaration line do the `fun`s
--
-- The spelling Lean users reach for most often puts the binders right after
-- the name:
--
--   theorem and_swap2 (a : Prop) (b : Prop) (h : And a b) : And b a := …
--
-- It is the same thing as the arrow spelling: the type is an arrow telescope
-- and the value gets the matching `fun`s wrapped around it automatically. The
-- difference shows up while writing the proof — the binders are already in
-- scope, so there are no `fun`s (and no `intro`) to write.
--
-- Demo: this declaration and Exercise 3's and_swap are the same problem in
-- two spellings; the kernel judges them identically.
theorem demo_binders (a : Prop) (b : Prop) (h : And a b) : And b a :=
  And.intro b a (And.right a b h) (And.left a b h)

-- Exercise 6: prove and_intro_rule again, this time with declaration binders
-- (no `fun`s to write).
-- soko:hint approach: a, b, ha and hb are all already in scope; assemble And a b directly.
-- soko:hint goal shape: the goal is just And a b — no arrows left to split, and no fun to write.
-- soko:hint key piece: And.intro a b ha hb closes it in one step.
theorem and_intro_rule2 (a : Prop) (b : Prop) (ha : a) (hb : b) : And a b := sorry